Brown Points to New Report That Found Ohio Women Employed Full Time are Paid Just 77 Cents for Every Dollar Paid to Men



			Tuesday, April 8, 2014



			WASHINGTON, D.C. – Today, on Equal Pay Day and in advance of a vote on paycheck fairness, U.S. Sen. Sherrod Brown (D-OH) called for passage of legislation that would close loopholes that allow pay discrimination based on gender. Brown also pointed towards a new study by the National Partnership for Women &amp; Families that found that women who are employed full time in Ohio are paid just 77 cents for every dollar paid to men, amounting to a yearly gap in wages of $10,805. In total, each year of unequal wages costs Ohio women nearly $17 billion in lost income.
“Women who provide equal work shouldn’t be subjected to unequal pay. Period,” Brown said. “But women working full-time earn only 77 cents to every dollar earned by a man. Just in Ohio, this leads to a staggering gap of nearly $17 billion every year. The Paycheck Fairness Act would help ensure women are paid the wage they’ve earned. This would protect their rights, allow them to better provide for their children, and contribute more to our local economies.”   
Equal Pay Day is annually held on April 8, exactly 23 percent into the calendar year; emblematic for women’s salaries that are 23 percent less than their male counterparts. By that measure of inequality, today marks the symbolic beginning of American women earning a paycheck for the 2014 calendar year. The Paycheck Fairness Act would strengthen the Equal Pay Act of 1963 and help women fight wage discrimination by:

Disallowing employers from retaliating against workers who, with their colleagues, discuss salaries;


Making gender-based discrimination as equal to other forms of wage discrimination, and allowing women to take legal action for damages;


Requiring employers to prove that pay differences exist for legitimate reasons such as education, training, and experience;


Creating a negotiation skills training program for women and girls;


Establishing the Secretary of Labor’s National Award for Pay Equity in the Workplace for an employer who has made a substantial effort to eliminate pay disparities between men and women;


Providing small businesses and other businesses assistance with equal pay practices; and


Enhancing the ability of the Department of Labor and the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission to investigate and enforce pay discrimination laws. 

The new analysis Brown pointed to by the National Partnership for Women &amp; Families concludes that if the gap between men’s and women’s wages in Ohio were eliminated, Ohio women working full-time could buy groceries for 1.7 more years; pay mortgage and utilities for nine more months; pay rent for 15 more months, or purchase 3,007 more gallons of gas.
This not only affects women, but the families of women as well. According to the Democratic Policy &amp; Communications Center (DPCC), more than 71 percent of women with children work, and about 40 percent of woman in households with children are either the sole or primary wage earner. 
